We introduce a novel laser-plasma accelerator capable of generating high-energy electron beams with a controllable longitudinal profile. This device utilizes a self-steepening laser beam to create a plasma wakefield, which is then modulated by the laser's polarization. We demonstrate that this approach can produce electron beams with a tunable energy spread. Furthermore, we show that the resulting electron beams can be accelerated to energies exceeding 1 GeV. These findings suggest that polarization regulation of the plasma wakefield offers a promising method for creating high-energy electron beams with adjustable energy spread. Additionally, this technique may enable the generation of high-energy, high-current positron beams. The proposed scheme is applicable to both gas and solid plasma targets. Our results provide a foundation for developing compact, high-energy, and high-current electron accelerators. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 11974290) and the National Key R&D Program of China (Grant No. 2018YFB0307900). Correspondence: email: zhangyong@iccas.ac.cn.
Keywords: Laser-plasma acceleration, electron beam, positron beam, polarization, self-steepening.
